# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the HYBRID_SANITIZE CI leg (issue #42).
#
# What this file can and cannot prove
# -----------------------------------
# The sanitizer build instruments the hybrid provider and its tests, but NOT
# OpenSSL, liboqs, or the PQ-only oqsprovider. The CI leg runs LSan with the
# default (fast, frame-pointer) unwinder, so every leak's allocation stack is
# captured only two frames deep: `[malloc, CRYPTO_malloc]`. Because ALL OpenSSL
# heap goes through CRYPTO_malloc (in libcrypto), `leak:libcrypto` then matches
# essentially every leak, whoever really owns it. So this file does NOT, on its
# own, distinguish a leak in the provider's code from one in a dependency — and a
# NEW provider leak reached from a test that does not unload its providers would
# be masked here. Do not treat a green LSan leg as proof of provider
# leak-freedom; that is established by the guardrails below, not by this file.
#
# Audit of what actually leaks (deep unwind: fast_unwind_on_malloc=0)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Re-running the suite with the slow unwinder gives full stacks and lets each
# leaked block be attributed to its DIRECT allocator (the first frame below
# CRYPTO_(m|z|re)alloc). Doing so across the encode/param/cms/composite tests
# shows exactly two classes:
#   1. Blocks whose direct allocator is a dependency (e.g. an EVP_SIGNATURE/
#      EVP_KEM method built by evp_*_from_algorithm and cached in the libctx
#      method store). These are libcrypto/liboqs-owned; a provider frame appears
#      only higher up as the call that first triggered the fetch (e.g. the cede
#      probe's EVP_SIGNATURE_fetch). Not ours to free.
#   2. Blocks whose direct allocator IS provider code — and these are ONLY the
#      three per-instance provctx allocations: the cede runtime tables
#      (hybrid_filter_algs), the ceded-name array (hybrid_apply_cede), and the
#      provctx struct itself (OSSL_provider_init). All three are freed by
#      hybrid_teardown(). NO key, operation, encode/decode, or sign/verify path
#      allocation appears in this class.
# Both classes surface for the SAME reason: those tests call OSSL_LIB_CTX_free
# without first OSSL_PROVIDER_unload-ing, and a provider's teardown does not run
# while the caller still holds its load reference — so teardown (and the libctx
# method-store cleanup) is deferred to OPENSSL_cleanup at process exit, which
# runs AFTER LSan's check.
#
# The real guardrails against a provider leak
# -------------------------------------------
#   1. hybrid_teardown() frees every provctx-owned allocation, and per-key /
#      per-operation state is freed in the corresponding *_free / *_freectx (the
#      subject of the issue #42 memory-safety audit + code review).
#   2. hybrid_leakcheck_test is the automated guard for FUTURE allocations: it
#      exercises and frees every provider allocation path (keygen/free, import/
#      export, match, sign/verify, encaps/decaps, encode/decode) across the whole
#      inventory, unloads every provider before freeing the libctx (so teardown
#      runs), and — crucially — is pinned to test/lsan_leakcheck.supp, which OMITS
#      `leak:libcrypto`. So a leak newly added to any exercised provider path is
#      NOT masked there and fails that test, even though it would be masked by the
#      `leak:libcrypto` rule below. (Demonstrated by injecting a deliberate leak:
#      hybrid_leakcheck_test catches it; this file's leg does not.)
#   3. Tests that unload their providers before freeing the libctx report ZERO
#      provider-owned leaks under LSan even with THIS FILE EMPTY. hybrid_adverse_
#      test and hybrid_leakcheck_test (issue #47) are the worked examples and the
#      pattern to follow; extending that unload discipline to the rest of the
#      suite would let these module suppressions be narrowed to the genuine
#      dependency-init leaks.
#
# In short: this file keeps the broad `leak:libcrypto` rule so the general leg
# stays green on the pre-existing un-unloaded tests, while hybrid_leakcheck_test
# (with lsan_leakcheck.supp) is where a new provider leak is actually caught.
#
# The four rules below silence the one-time init state of each un-instrumented
# dependency — state it allocates on first use and releases only at process exit.

# OpenSSL core: provider store, default library context, DRBG, error/OBJ/config
# tables, and cached EVP_* methods in a libctx method store — released at
# OPENSSL_cleanup, after LSan's end-of-run check.
leak:libcrypto

# libssl: global TLS init state (method/ciphersuite tables, SSL_CTX internals)
# reached via the SSL_CTX_new / TLS-init phases; same process-exit lifetime.
leak:libssl

# liboqs: global algorithm/OID tables initialized on first PQ operation; freed at
# process exit, not per-libctx.
leak:liboqs

# oqsprovider: the PQ-only provider we load for interop is a separate,
# un-instrumented module; its own provctx/init state is not ours to free.
leak:oqsprovider
